Lensky actually froze samples of the bacteria every 500 generations, creating a literal frozen

08:06.320 --> 08:09.540
fossil record that could be thawed out and sequenced later.

08:10.040 --> 08:14.560
By the time Flyxion is citing this data, we are looking at over 30,000 generations of bacteria.

08:14.840 --> 08:20.040
To put that timescale into human perspective, if you trace human generations back 30,000 times,

08:20.520 --> 08:25.260
you are looking at early Homo sapiens wandering the plains of Africa roughly three quarters of

08:25.260 --> 08:26.040
a million years ago.

08:26.040 --> 08:26.880
Exactly.

08:27.520 --> 08:32.160
In Lensky's lab, this immense stretch of evolutionary time happens over a few decades.

08:32.760 --> 08:39.520
Across these 12 flasks, billions upon billions of random genetic mutations are tested by natural

08:39.520 --> 08:40.020
selection.

08:40.360 --> 08:44.380
And what happens after all that time is the absolute foundation affliction's argument.

08:44.720 --> 08:51.200
Because over 30,000 generations, the researchers track the DNA and the physical traits of all

08:51.200 --> 08:52.480
12 populations.

08:52.480 --> 08:59.200
And what they find is that 11 of those 12 populations converge on very similar adaptive trajectories.

08:59.540 --> 09:02.520
Yes, they all evolve to get much better at eating limited glucose.

09:03.100 --> 09:06.120
Their individual cell sizes increase in almost identical ways.

09:06.360 --> 09:08.560
They alter their gene expression similarly.

09:08.980 --> 09:13.620
They essentially arrive at the exact same biological solutions to the problem of the glucose-limited

09:13.620 --> 09:14.100
flask.

09:14.380 --> 09:17.480
But, and this is the kicker, none of them learn to eat the citrate.

09:17.580 --> 09:18.480
Right, except for one.

09:18.480 --> 09:24.300
Yes, in exactly one population, just one out of the 12, specifically the population Lensky

09:24.300 --> 09:30.780
labeled Era 3, a variant, appears around generation 31,000 that suddenly miraculously figures out

09:30.780 --> 09:32.120
how to metabolize the citrate.

09:32.200 --> 09:33.760
It's an incredible moment in biology.

09:34.040 --> 09:39.700
The liquid in that one flask suddenly turns cloudy as the bacteria absolutely explode in

09:39.700 --> 09:43.160
population, gorging on the food source the others can't touch.

09:43.520 --> 09:45.640
One diverges completely from the pack.

09:45.740 --> 09:51.980
What's fascinating here is the absolute sheer explanatory failure of the common ancestor.

09:52.700 --> 09:54.700
Think back to the historical origin, the OH.

09:54.980 --> 09:56.820
Right, the 1988 bacteria.

09:56.880 --> 09:57.140
Yes.

09:57.480 --> 10:00.900
All 12 populations share the exact same historical origin.

10:00.900 --> 10:04.580
They literally came from the same single bacterial cell in 1988.

10:05.820 --> 10:10.840
If shared ancestry, the lineage assumption is what explains why species arrive at similar

10:10.840 --> 10:13.680
outcomes, then how on earth you explain this result?

10:13.720 --> 10:14.680
You really can't.

10:14.820 --> 10:20.000
Ancestry is a mathematical constant across all 12 flasks, yet 11 converged on one solution

10:20.000 --> 10:21.620
and one radically diverged.

10:21.780 --> 10:23.740
The constant cannot explain the variation.

10:23.920 --> 10:26.360
Right, it completely shatters the photo album logic.

10:26.360 --> 10:30.620
If the great-grandfather's DNA is the only reason the kids look the same, then all the

10:30.620 --> 10:32.020
kids should look exactly the same.

10:32.340 --> 10:37.440
But here, the grandfather's DNA is totally useless as an explanation for why 11 converged

10:37.440 --> 10:39.520
and one became a mutant super eater.

10:39.780 --> 10:41.880
Flyxion introduces a formal term for this.

10:42.060 --> 10:43.200
