It is a completely predictable mathematical consequence of a narrow admissibility field acting

16:29.460 --> 16:30.820
on independent trajectories.

16:31.000 --> 16:32.300
Okay, let's take a breath.

16:32.720 --> 16:37.540
Because Lenski's bacteria beautifully prove that an ancestor can be physically present,

16:37.980 --> 16:41.580
but entirely causally irrelevant to the outcome.

16:41.820 --> 16:42.720
It's screened off.

16:42.720 --> 16:49.460
But that is all happening inside a highly sanitized, completely controlled, microscopic glass environment

16:49.460 --> 16:52.780
over a relatively short period of human observation.

16:52.820 --> 16:52.980
Right.

16:53.180 --> 16:54.520
It's a very specific scenario.

16:54.760 --> 16:55.620
I want to zoom out.

16:55.860 --> 16:57.480
What happens when we leave the lab?

16:57.920 --> 17:03.080
What happens when we look at the massive, messy, chaotic scale of global evolutionary time

17:03.080 --> 17:04.460
over tens of millions of years?

17:04.460 --> 17:09.800
This is where Flyxion shifts from the concept of screening off to a far more profound concept

17:09.800 --> 17:11.620
they call deep absence.

17:11.980 --> 17:12.640
Deep absence?

17:13.020 --> 17:14.100
That sounds intense.

17:14.240 --> 17:18.220
If Lenski's experiment is a case where the shared ancestor is present but irrelevant,

17:18.660 --> 17:23.680
macroevolution provides us with stunning cases where the shared ancestor is completely undesirably

17:23.680 --> 17:24.040
absent.

17:24.180 --> 17:25.620
It literally does not exist.

17:25.740 --> 17:28.640
And yet the exact same structural convergence happens anyway.

17:29.140 --> 17:33.440
Which brings us to one of my absolute favorite weird phenomena in the natural world.

17:33.440 --> 17:35.060
I think I know where you're going with this.

17:35.440 --> 17:39.440
If you spend any time scrolling through the science side of the internet, you have probably

17:39.440 --> 17:40.880
seen memes about this.

17:41.080 --> 17:42.840
We are talking about carcinization.

17:43.100 --> 17:43.320
Yes.

17:43.840 --> 17:49.920
The seemingly unstoppable biological tendency for various crustaceans to independently turn

17:49.920 --> 17:50.620
into crabs.

17:50.740 --> 17:52.440
It is so incredibly weird and wonderful.

17:52.940 --> 17:58.920
The term carcinization was actually coined way back in 1916 by an evolutionary zeologist named

17:58.920 --> 17:59.920
L.A. Bordale.

17:59.920 --> 18:02.180
Studying decapod crustaceans, right?

18:02.180 --> 18:02.300
Yep.

18:02.680 --> 18:08.200
And he literally described this phenomenon as nature's many attempts to arrive at a crab.

18:08.360 --> 18:13.120
To understand why this is such a devastating piece of evidence for Flyxion's admissibility

18:13.120 --> 18:17.200
field, we have to establish what a crab actually is, biologically speaking.

18:17.300 --> 18:18.920
It's not just like a vibe.

18:19.100 --> 18:20.260
No, it's not a vibe at all.

18:20.680 --> 18:24.620
It's a very specific, highly engineered set of physical constraints.

18:24.620 --> 18:31.840
True crabs, or brachyura, possess a flattened, dorsoventrally compressed carapace, meaning

18:31.840 --> 18:34.560
their shell looks squished flat from top to bottom.

18:35.000 --> 18:41.040
They have a drastically reduced abdomen, or pleon, that is permanently folded up tightly

18:41.040 --> 18:45.360
underneath their body, completely hidden from view, and they have a widened sternum.

18:45.880 --> 18:52.340
It is a highly specific, highly defensive form of armor designed for a very specific way

18:52.340 --> 18:52.820
of moving.

18:52.820 --> 18:53.300
Right.

18:53.480 --> 18:57.040
When a kid draws a crab on the beach, they are drawing those specific traits.

18:57.220 --> 19:00.760
But here is the mind-blowing part that breaks the lineage assumption.

19:01.640 --> 19:05.520
True crabs are not the only things in the ocean that look exactly like crabs.

19:05.680 --> 19:06.300
Not even close.

19:06.500 --> 19:10.680
Nature has produced a whole slew of false crabs belonging to the group enomera.

19:11.420 --> 19:15.580
This includes things like porcelain crabs, king crabs, and hairy stone crabs.

19:16.040 --> 19:19.680
If you look at a king crab, it has the flat shell, it has the tucked under abdomen, it's

19:19.680 --> 19:20.140
a crab.

19:20.140 --> 19:24.800
But when evolutionary biologists actually map out the genetic family tree of all these

19:24.800 --> 19:30.140
decapods, these 10-legged crustaceans, they find that this exact, highly specific crab

19:30.140 --> 19:35.340
body plan has evolved completely independently at least five separate times.

19:35.440 --> 19:36.240
Five different times.

19:36.600 --> 19:36.900
Yes.

19:37.760 --> 19:42.640
Five different branches of the family tree, separated by tens of millions of years of

19:42.640 --> 19:48.140
evolutionary history, all woke up one epoch and essentially decided, you know what, we

19:48.140 --> 19:48.900
should be crabs.

19:49.460 --> 19:56.140
Which means that if you take a true crab and a king crab and you trace their genetic lineages

19:56.140 --> 20:00.220
back, back, back in time to their most recent common ancestor.

20:00.220 --> 20:04.000
That ancestor was absolutely manifestly not a crab.

20:04.140 --> 20:04.380
Right.

20:04.840 --> 20:10.620
The fossil record indicates it was likely something long, squishy, and vaguely lobster-like or

20:10.620 --> 20:11.320
shrimp-like.

20:11.680 --> 20:16.220
The crab shape is not a biological baton that was passed down from a crab grandfather.

20:16.460 --> 20:21.260
It is a morphological destination that was arrived at completely independently from entirely

20:21.260 --> 20:22.820
different biological starting points.

20:22.960 --> 20:23.060
Yeah.

20:23.060 --> 20:24.640
This is Flyxion's deep absence.

20:25.120 --> 20:29.060
So the explanatory origin, the reason they are crabs, is completely divorced from the

20:29.060 --> 20:32.420
historical origin, their long-dead, non-crab ancestor.

20:32.580 --> 20:33.060
Exactly.

20:33.380 --> 20:37.340
Okay, I'm going to put on my skeptic hat and push back hard here because I know what some

20:37.340 --> 20:39.820
of you listening are probably screaming at your devices right now.

20:39.880 --> 20:40.420
Let's hear it.

20:40.720 --> 20:46.440
Couldn't they just have inherited some kind of dormant crab toolkit from that deep, ancient,

20:46.440 --> 20:47.960
shrimp-like ancestor?

20:47.960 --> 20:54.240
Like, a compressed ZIP file of crab DNA that just sat unused on their genetic hard drive

20:54.240 --> 20:58.020
for millions of years until some environmental trigger unzipped it?

20:58.420 --> 21:03.960
Isn't this just standard convergent evolution where the basic biological machinery is shared

21:03.960 --> 21:06.200
deep down so, of course, they build the same house?

21:06.340 --> 21:08.640
It's an excellent, intuitive pushback.

21:08.920 --> 21:12.680
And it's one Flyxion addresses explicitly in the paper to head off critics.

21:12.900 --> 21:13.040
Yeah.

21:13.040 --> 21:13.980
Because you are right.

21:13.980 --> 21:18.560
But some developmental biologists do suggest that these various crustacean lineages might

21:18.560 --> 21:21.840
share some deep, underlying gene regulatory networks, a toolkit.

21:22.240 --> 21:23.680
So maybe there is a shared blueprint.

21:24.020 --> 21:28.840
But even if we totally grant that there is a dormant crab toolkit buried deep in their DNA,

21:29.460 --> 21:34.800
the researchers who actively study carcinization emphatically reject the idea that evolution

21:34.800 --> 21:38.240
is somehow teleologically aiming at an ideal crab.

21:38.560 --> 21:40.840
Teleologically meaning it has an end goal in mind.

21:40.840 --> 21:44.680
Right. Teleology is the idea that there is a predetermined end goal.

21:45.240 --> 21:47.460
Having bricks doesn't mean you are destined to build a chimney.

21:47.980 --> 21:53.180
So if it's not a genetic destiny programmed into the DNA, how do five different lineages

21:53.180 --> 21:55.940
all end up building the exact same biological chimney?

21:56.820 --> 21:59.620
It is the admissibility field of the ocean floor.

22:00.260 --> 22:07.060
The benthic environment presents a very narrow, very specific set of physical, hydrodynamic,

22:07.180 --> 22:08.400
and predatory constraints.

22:08.740 --> 22:09.200
Like what?

22:09.200 --> 22:14.400
Well, if you are a crustacean trying to survive on the bottom of the ocean, moving sideways,

22:14.880 --> 22:21.240
hiding in rock crevices, and avoiding predators attacking from above, being flat, heavily armored,

22:21.580 --> 22:28.040
and having the ability to tuck your soft, vulnerable abdomen away is a wildly successful survival strategy.

22:28.300 --> 22:31.200
Ah, so it's just the best design for that specific neighborhood.

22:31.420 --> 22:31.880
Exactly.

22:31.880 --> 22:37.820
The admissibility field of the benthic zone heavily favors that specific region of biological real estate.

22:38.440 --> 22:43.580
The various independent lineages of true crabs and false crabs didn't read the same genetic blueprint.

22:43.920 --> 22:48.160
They just all bumped into the exact same environmental walls over millions of years,

22:48.400 --> 22:52.680
and natural selection brutally squeezed them into the exact same highly efficient shape.

22:52.680 --> 22:56.520
They all just blindly fumbled around in the dark until they found the trapdoor in the escape room,

22:56.620 --> 22:58.200
just millions of years apart.

22:58.600 --> 22:59.120
Precisely.

22:59.760 --> 23:03.940
And to truly drive a stake through the heart of the genetic destiny argument,

23:04.500 --> 23:07.420
Flyxion brings up a fascinating, almost comical counterpoint.

23:07.960 --> 23:12.960
The field isn't an inescapable black hole pulling all crustaceans toward crabhood forever.

23:12.960 --> 23:16.640
However, there's a documented phenomenon called decarcinization.

23:16.980 --> 23:17.460
Wait, I'm sorry.

23:17.600 --> 23:21.500
Things actively evolving away from being crabs, uncrabbing themselves.

23:21.640 --> 23:22.860
Yes, exactly that.

23:23.000 --> 23:23.740
No way.

23:23.840 --> 23:27.920
The fossil and genetic record shows that the highly evolved crab body plan

23:27.920 --> 23:32.520
has been subsequently lost at least seven separate times in evolutionary history.

23:32.700 --> 23:33.740
Seven times.

23:34.160 --> 23:38.900
Some lineages successfully became crabs, existed as crabs for millions of years,

23:38.900 --> 23:43.860
and then later, due to shifting environmental pressures or changes in their own localized behavior,

23:44.320 --> 23:45.420
they uncrabbed themselves.

23:45.900 --> 23:51.060
They reverted back to more elongated, asymmetric, lobster-like, or hermit-crab-like forms.

23:51.200 --> 23:52.200
That is wild.

23:52.300 --> 23:54.860
I feel like reverting from a crab is a huge downgrade.

23:55.100 --> 23:59.580
It might seem like it, but it is a crucial piece of structural evidence for Flyxion's argument.

24:00.600 --> 24:03.780
Decarcinization proves that the admissibility field is not a magical,

24:04.200 --> 24:08.100
one-way teleological ratchet forcing a predetermined genetic outcome.

24:08.100 --> 24:09.600
It's not a one-way street.

24:09.740 --> 24:09.840
Right.

24:10.180 --> 24:12.860
It is simply a structured space of probabilities.

24:13.820 --> 24:17.760
Lineages can enter the crab region of the field when their local history and environment

24:17.760 --> 24:19.620
make it advantageous and reachable,

24:19.900 --> 24:24.060
and they can exit that region when new environmental pathways open up.

24:24.260 --> 24:28.780
It completely destroys the idea that convergence requires a shared destiny

24:28.780 --> 24:32.440
or a shared historical ancestor dictating the outcome.

24:32.920 --> 24:36.080
The environment, the constraints, is the true author of the organism.

24:36.320 --> 24:37.240
It's all about the field.

24:37.240 --> 24:43.300
Okay, so we've looked at this phenomenon happening in a microscopic glass flask over decades.

24:43.600 --> 24:47.700
We've looked at it happening on the ocean floor over tens of millions of years.

24:47.920 --> 24:50.720
But now we're going to take a hard left turn.

24:51.100 --> 24:53.360
We're leaving biology entirely behind.

24:53.560 --> 24:53.980
We are.

24:54.100 --> 24:56.360
We are going to step into the realm of human thought,

24:56.560 --> 25:01.540
because this is the moment in Flyxion's paper where it stops being just a cool science lesson about animals

25:01.540 --> 25:04.680
and starts making you deeply question the nature of your own brain.

25:04.680 --> 25:10.020
If biological bodies are constrained by the physical reality of the ocean floor,

25:10.220 --> 25:11.960
what constrains a human thought?

25:12.480 --> 25:13.920
How do ideas form?

25:14.320 --> 25:20.740
According to Flyxion, human thought is constrained by the exact same mathematical principles of the admissibility field.

25:21.400 --> 25:27.080
Cultural and intellectual convergence operates under the same structural laws as biological convergence.

25:27.080 --> 25:35.780
And to demonstrate this, Flyxion points to perhaps the most famous, most dramatic instance of deep absence in all of intellectual history,

25:36.300 --> 25:43.720
the simultaneous independent discovery of the theory of evolution by natural selection by Charles Darwin and Alfred Russell Wallace.

25:43.980 --> 25:45.080
It's an incredible story.

25:45.420 --> 25:47.140
This story is like a Victorian thriller.

25:47.140 --> 25:49.680
Listeners, put yourself in Charles Darwin's shoes.

25:49.820 --> 25:51.220
It's the late 1830s.

25:51.520 --> 25:54.940
He's back in the UK after his five-year voyage on the HMS Beagle.

25:55.160 --> 25:56.720
He's collected all these amazing specimens.

25:56.900 --> 25:57.160
Right.

25:57.480 --> 26:01.480
He's looking at his finches, his tortoises, his obsessive cataloging of barnacles.

26:01.700 --> 26:09.280
And he slowly pieces together this massive paradigm-shattering epiphany about how species change over time.

26:09.560 --> 26:12.380
But Darwin is intensely aware of the society he lives in.

26:12.380 --> 26:17.980
He is terrified of the social, scientific, and specifically the religious blowback that this theory will cause.

26:18.380 --> 26:19.040
So what does he do?

26:19.280 --> 26:20.100
He hoards it.

26:20.480 --> 26:25.240
He writes it down in private notebooks, locks it in a desk drawer at his country estate,

26:25.580 --> 26:33.560
and only whispers about it to a tiny, incredibly trusted inner circle of friends like the geologist Charles Lyell and the botanist Joseph Hooker.

26:33.920 --> 26:39.000
He sits on the biggest idea in human history for 20 years.

26:39.400 --> 26:40.760
20 years.

26:40.760 --> 26:45.100
During which time, he genuinely believes he is the sole proprietor of this idea.

26:45.660 --> 26:47.380
He's the singular historical origin.

26:47.440 --> 26:47.640
Right.

26:47.720 --> 26:48.960
He thinks he owns the timeline.

26:49.700 --> 26:59.840
But then, fast forward to June 1858, Darwin goes to his mailbox and pulls out a package from a younger, far less established naturalist named Alfred Russell Wallace.

27:00.200 --> 27:02.260
And Wallace is in a completely different situation.

27:02.400 --> 27:02.660
Totally.

27:02.660 --> 27:14.580
Wallace is currently out in the Malay archipelago, modern-day Indonesia, sweating through brutal bouts of malaria, catching beetles and birds of paradise to sell to wealthy collectors back in England to fund his expeditions.

27:15.000 --> 27:18.860
Wallace has absolutely zero access to Darwin's secret locked notebooks.

27:19.260 --> 27:21.200
He is not in the inner circle.

27:21.200 --> 27:26.620
And yet, Darwin opens this manuscript and his jaw practically hits the floor.

27:27.040 --> 27:31.540
Because Wallace has just articulated the exact same mechanism of natural selection.

27:31.720 --> 27:32.660
It's virtually identical.

27:32.940 --> 27:41.140
It is so identical in its logic and structure that Darwin writes to his friends in an absolute panic, essentially saying,

27:41.140 --> 28:01.660
And what's incredibly poignant is that neither man was even in the room when their ideas fundamentally changed the scientific world forever.

28:01.660 --> 28:06.000
Right. Wallace was thousands of miles away in a jungle, completely unaware of what was happening.

28:06.160 --> 28:11.900
And Darwin was at home mourning the tragic death of his infant son from scarlet fever just days prior.

28:12.120 --> 28:16.420
But the underlying intellectual mechanics of what happened here are exactly what Flyxion is trying to eliminate.

28:16.580 --> 28:17.700
There was no transmission event.

28:17.780 --> 28:18.360
There was no copying.

28:18.700 --> 28:23.100
There was no shared ancestor for this specific articulation of the theory.

28:23.780 --> 28:28.980
Darwin did not whisper it to Wallace across the ocean, and Wallace did not plagiarize it from Darwin.

28:28.980 --> 28:37.560
So the obvious question is, how do two completely different guys, separated by half a planet and completely different life circumstances,

28:38.220 --> 28:43.940
arrive at the exact same world-altering thought at the exact same moment in history?

28:44.480 --> 28:46.460
Because they shared an intellectual field.

28:47.000 --> 28:55.700
They were both fully immersed in an intellectual admissibility field that made the theory of natural selection not just possible, but highly reachable, almost inevitable.

28:56.100 --> 28:57.860
What were the constraints of that field?

28:57.860 --> 29:03.600
Specifically, both men were deeply influenced by reading Thomas Malthus' essay on population pressure.

29:03.960 --> 29:12.160
Malthus argued that human populations grow exponentially, but the food supply only grows arithmetically, leading to an inevitable, brutal struggle for existence.

29:12.400 --> 29:16.120
Ah, so they both took that economic idea and applied it to biology.

29:16.560 --> 29:17.100
Precisely.

29:17.100 --> 29:25.980
Furthermore, both men were swimming in a rapidly accumulating shared sea of new natural history evidence gathered from global imperial expeditions.

29:26.380 --> 29:30.800
And both were reading Lyell's Principles of Geology, which established deep time.

29:31.000 --> 29:33.620
So the environment was basically forcing them into this funnel.

29:33.620 --> 29:42.880
That specific intellectual environment, that specific moment in Victorian science, rigidly constrained what theories were logically viable.

29:43.500 --> 29:46.160
It formed a narrow intellectual corridor.

29:47.160 --> 29:48.520
Two independent trajectories.

29:49.280 --> 29:51.220
Darwin, in his comfortable English study,

29:51.620 --> 29:57.900
Wallace, in his fever-soaked jungle hut, walked down that corridor and bumped into the exact same conceptual wall.

29:57.900 --> 30:00.960
The constraints of the field dictated the reachable solution.

30:01.640 --> 30:04.700
So if we pull back, what does this all actually mean for human innovation?

30:04.880 --> 30:06.220
Let me try another analogy here.

30:06.440 --> 30:13.720
Because expanding this from two individual Victorian scientists to entire global civilizations is the next massive step Flyxion takes.

30:13.840 --> 30:14.900
Okay, let's hear the analogy.

30:15.280 --> 30:23.200
Imagine a scenario where multiple completely isolated civilizations scattered around the globe with no contact whatsoever.

30:23.200 --> 30:28.140
And in the exact same century, they all independently invent the umbrella.

30:28.340 --> 30:29.500
A classic problem.

30:29.660 --> 30:34.000
Now, the old lineage assumption way of thinking, the photo album logic, would say,

30:34.520 --> 30:39.100
oh, well, there must have been one guy, an umbrella patient zero, who invented it in one village,

30:39.420 --> 30:45.140
and then he hopped on a boat and spent his life sailing around the world teaching every other culture how to make umbrellas.

30:45.140 --> 30:53.500
Which is a theory that requires an incredibly complex, highly unlikely, and often entirely undocumented historical transmission chain.

30:53.940 --> 30:58.620
It bends over backwards to preserve the idea of a single historical origin.

30:58.780 --> 30:59.220
Exactly.

30:59.380 --> 31:01.120
It's an exhausting way to view history.

31:01.900 --> 31:06.960
But Flyxion's view, the explanatory origin view, is far simpler and far more profound.

31:07.460 --> 31:14.040
What if a massive post-glacial climate shift happened and it just started raining heavily and constantly all over the globe?

31:14.040 --> 31:15.000
The field changes.

31:15.360 --> 31:15.660
Right.

31:16.020 --> 31:19.320
The constraint, the rain, dictates the reachable solution.

31:20.340 --> 31:26.920
Humans want to stay dry to preserve body heat, but they also need their hands-free to carry food and tools.

31:26.920 --> 31:38.080
A stick with a waterproof canopy on top of it is the most admissible, geometrically sound solution to that specific environmental constraint given primitive materials.

31:38.820 --> 31:40.320
The rain invented the umbrella.

31:40.320 --> 31:45.340
Yes. The individual civilizations just happen to be the trajectories standing in the rain.

31:45.700 --> 31:56.220
And while the umbrella is a great conceptual metaphor, what you've just articulated is a perfect synthesis of Flyxion's next major real-world historical example,

31:56.740 --> 31:59.060
the independent origins of human agriculture.

31:59.220 --> 32:00.400
Oh, this is a big one.

32:00.400 --> 32:11.800
It is. If Darwin and Wallace represent two isolated individuals converging, the dawn of agriculture represents entire massive human societies converging on the exact same economic solution.

32:11.920 --> 32:21.580
Right. If you look at the archaeological and historical record, it shows unequivocally that the domestication of plants and animals didn't happen in one specific place and then spread outward like a ripple in a pond.

32:21.580 --> 32:22.160
No, it didn't.

32:22.320 --> 32:25.920
It happened completely independently in at least five major centers.

32:26.580 --> 32:36.720
The Fertile Crescent in the Middle East, the Yangtze and Yellow River basins in China, Mesoamerica, the Andes and Amazonia in South America, and the highlands of New Guinea.

32:36.860 --> 32:41.960
All separated by thousands of miles of impassable oceans, deserts, and mountain ranges.

32:42.180 --> 32:45.920
There were no transcontinental trade routes connecting them all 10,000 years ago.

32:45.920 --> 33:00.340
And there was no agricultural patient zero. There was no single Neolithic genius who figured out how to domesticate wild einkorn wheat in Syria and then decided to walk thousands of miles to Peru to tell them how to domesticate potatoes.

33:00.540 --> 33:01.760
So what actually happened?

33:01.860 --> 33:08.600
What actually happened was the arrival of a shared global constraint hitting different geographies at roughly the same time via a common cause.

33:09.080 --> 33:14.780
The extreme post-glacial climate stabilization that occurred in the early to mid-Holocene epoch.

33:14.780 --> 33:16.140
Okay, so the Ice Age ends.

33:16.420 --> 33:16.800
Exactly.

33:17.720 --> 33:24.980
As the last Ice Age receded, the global climate became significantly warmer, wetter, and crucially, more predictable.

33:25.820 --> 33:28.920
However, resource availability drastically shifted.

33:29.440 --> 33:30.440
Megafauna died out.

33:31.060 --> 33:37.200
Hunter-gatherer populations across the globe were suddenly phasing highly comparable pressures on their subsistence strategies.

33:37.540 --> 33:40.140
The rains started falling, metaphorically speaking.

33:40.600 --> 33:41.980
The environment changed the rules.

33:41.980 --> 33:49.560
Precisely. Entire human populations were pushed out of their old survival strategies and into a brand new admissibility field.

33:50.260 --> 34:00.600
And in that new, stable, but pressured field, the most reachable, viable, high-yield solution to stabilizing a food supply was the intense cultivation of local wild flora.

34:00.800 --> 34:03.140
So it didn't matter what specific plant they were looking at.

34:03.140 --> 34:10.000
No, it genuinely did not matter if you were a population looking at wild Tiacente grass in ancient Mexico or wild rice in ancient China.

34:10.520 --> 34:14.320
The underlying structural constraints of the survival problem were mathematically identical.

34:14.880 --> 34:19.720
Agriculture is simply carcinization scaled up to the macro level of human economics and sociology.

34:20.200 --> 34:21.800
Wow, agriculture is just crabs.

34:21.800 --> 34:27.160
Exactly. It is not one brilliant lineage branching outward to save the world.

34:27.660 --> 34:35.740
It is several independent human trajectories being squeezed relentlessly into the exact same region of a constraint space.

34:36.060 --> 34:41.040
This framing is just, it really reframes how you view the entire arc of human history.

34:41.380 --> 34:47.820
It makes history feel far less like a chaotic series of random dominoes falling wherever the wind blows them,

34:48.140 --> 34:50.660
and much more like water flowing down a mountain.

34:50.660 --> 34:51.920
That's a great way to put it.

34:52.020 --> 34:59.080
The water doesn't know where it's going, but gravity and the shape of the rock ensure it will inevitably find the same valleys again and again.

34:59.520 --> 35:04.680
Which actually transitions us seamlessly into the next massive layer of Flyxion's argument.

35:04.900 --> 35:06.960
Right, because this observation isn't entirely new.

35:07.080 --> 35:12.660
No. Flyxion isn't the first person to notice this feeling of inevitability that history seems to repeat itself.

35:13.020 --> 35:13.180
Yeah.

35:13.280 --> 35:19.460
There has been a massive, decades-long, incredibly vicious academic blood feud about exactly this concept.

