Accusations of plagiarism and theft flew back and forth across the English Channel.

53:01.760 --> 53:06.980
The Royal Society in London literally convened an official, supposedly impartial inquiry to settle,

53:07.320 --> 53:10.360
once and for all, who the true inventor of calculus was.

53:10.480 --> 53:12.040
And let's not forget the punchline here.

53:12.100 --> 53:12.540
Oh, I know.

53:12.540 --> 53:18.500
The president of the Royal Society at the time who oversaw this impartial inquiry was Isaac Newton.

53:18.500 --> 53:24.140
He literally convened an inquiry into his own dispute, secretly wrote the final record himself,

53:24.620 --> 53:27.060
and shockingly found completely in favor of himself.

53:27.420 --> 53:29.400
It is wild historical corruption.

53:29.660 --> 53:33.460
But the true tragedy of the Royal Society inquiry, as Flyxion points out,

53:33.900 --> 53:35.980
is not just the blatant ethical corruption.

53:36.420 --> 53:38.580
It's the massive intellectual blind spot.

53:39.320 --> 53:44.640
The inquiry spent a colossal amount of institutional energy desperately trying to adjudicate the OH,

53:44.780 --> 53:45.780
the historical origin.

53:45.780 --> 53:47.780
Who wrote it down in a notepic first?

53:48.100 --> 53:50.200
What specific date was stamped on this letter?

53:50.620 --> 53:55.260
Who visited whose study and saw whose papers on what Tuesday in 1673?

53:55.540 --> 53:57.340
We were utterly obsessed with the timeline.

53:57.580 --> 54:01.780
While completely willfully ignoring the OE, the explanatory origin.

54:02.180 --> 54:02.780
Exactly.

54:03.160 --> 54:08.060
They spent zero time asking the actually interesting, scientifically profound question.

54:08.680 --> 54:13.880
Why was calculus suddenly discoverable by two different men in the exact same decade?

54:13.880 --> 54:20.260
What was it about the antecedent mathematical field built by Fermat and Barrow that narrowed

54:20.260 --> 54:25.240
the intellectual constraints so tightly that the invention of calculus became the inevitable

54:25.240 --> 54:29.560
next step for anyone standing at those specific intellectual coordinates?

54:29.860 --> 54:32.100
They completely missed the point of how knowledge advances.

54:32.520 --> 54:37.420
It just shows that our society uses the concept of authorship entirely as a social convention.

54:37.420 --> 54:43.600
It's a pragmatic way to allocate money, grant prestige, and decide whose name goes on the Wikipedia entry.

54:43.940 --> 54:49.920
But it is a terrible, fundamentally flawed way to explain why an idea actually exists in the universe.

54:50.560 --> 54:52.980
Originality doesn't just mean being the first guy with a stopwatch.

54:53.060 --> 54:53.600
Not at all.

54:53.600 --> 54:59.620
Alfred Russell Wallace was just as original a thinker as Charles Darwin because Wallace actually hiked

54:59.620 --> 55:04.480
through the intellectual jungle of the admissibility field, suffered the constraints, and found the

55:04.480 --> 55:05.920
answer using his own mind.

55:06.360 --> 55:07.280
He didn't copy it.

55:07.540 --> 55:09.540
He just got to the finish line a few weeks later.

55:10.120 --> 55:14.060
To say Darwin is the origin of Wallace's idea is absurd.

55:14.060 --> 55:19.500
This leads to what is arguably the most quoted line of Flyxion's entire paper.

55:20.120 --> 55:22.820
They offer a new definition of true originality.

55:22.840 --> 55:23.680
I love this line.

55:23.800 --> 55:24.520
Flyxion writes,

55:25.080 --> 55:28.260
Originality is a relation between a trajectory and a field.

55:29.020 --> 55:32.140
Priority is merely a relation between a trajectory and a clock.

55:33.060 --> 55:34.120
They're not the same thing.

55:34.240 --> 55:36.140
I really need to pause and let that sink in.

55:36.500 --> 55:39.140
Originality is a relation between a trajectory and a field.

55:39.800 --> 55:42.240
Priority is a relation between a trajectory and a clock.

55:42.240 --> 55:46.060
That is such a profound, clarifying way to look at human endeavor.

55:46.440 --> 55:51.540
It frees us from this weird, crushing anxiety of influence, this fear that if someone else

55:51.540 --> 55:54.120
thought of it first, our own thoughts are somehow invalid.

55:54.420 --> 55:59.080
But it also raises a really grounding question, and this is where we need to temper the mind

55:59.080 --> 56:00.840
expansion with a little bit of harsh reality.

56:00.840 --> 56:01.580
Oh, definitely.

56:01.780 --> 56:05.840
Because, let's be honest, sometimes people do just steal things, right?

56:06.680 --> 56:07.600
Plagiarism is real.

56:08.040 --> 56:08.880
Of course it is.

56:08.880 --> 56:15.940
And Flyxion is very careful, as any good theoretician must be, not to overreach and claim that everything

56:15.940 --> 56:17.280
is independent convergence.

56:17.980 --> 56:23.680
If we connect this abstract theory to the messy reality of the bigger picture, the paper

56:23.680 --> 56:27.380
includes a crucial mathematical reality check near the end.

56:27.380 --> 56:33.000
The core question Flyxion addresses isn't whether lineage explanations tracing copies are always

56:33.000 --> 56:33.420
wrong.

56:33.820 --> 56:37.900
It's how we rigorously, scientifically know when they are right.

56:38.040 --> 56:38.240
Right.

56:38.320 --> 56:40.120
We don't want to throw the baby out with the bathwater.

56:40.240 --> 56:44.320
If someone blatantly rips off the script for this deep dive word for word, I can't have

56:44.320 --> 56:47.360
them going to the judge and saying, Your Honor, I didn't copy it.

56:47.620 --> 56:52.720
My client and the creators were simply navigating the exact same admissibility field of audio entertainment

56:52.720 --> 56:53.300
constraints.

56:53.660 --> 56:54.220
Precisely.

56:54.840 --> 56:59.700
To prevent the theory from being abused as an excuse for obvious theft, Flyxion introduces

56:59.700 --> 57:01.360
a simple mathematical comparison.

57:01.460 --> 57:02.340
Okay, lay it on us.

57:02.660 --> 57:07.880
In any given case of observed similarity between two things, you must impartially weigh two

57:07.880 --> 57:09.360
likelihoods against each other.

57:09.680 --> 57:14.740
You look at L lineage, the probability that this similarity is the result of a direct historical

57:14.740 --> 57:16.160
copy or shared ancestor.

57:16.160 --> 57:21.480
And you weigh it against LA, the probability that this is independent convergence dictated

57:21.480 --> 57:23.400
by a shared admissibility field.

57:24.240 --> 57:29.260
The scientific method demands you ask, Which hypothesis renders the observed resemblance

57:29.260 --> 57:30.020
more probable?

57:30.400 --> 57:31.580
Okay, so what is the tiebreaker?

57:31.980 --> 57:34.700
How do we know when L lineage decisively wins the fight?

57:35.100 --> 57:39.780
Lineage wins decisively when a direct transmission channel is not only theoretically plausible, but

57:39.780 --> 57:41.140
actively documentable.

57:41.140 --> 57:46.820
Frixian uses the spread of internet memes as a perfect, modern example of a domain where

57:46.820 --> 57:49.100
lineage absolutely dominates the explanation.

57:49.520 --> 57:52.480
Yeah, internet culture is a fascinating testing ground for this.

57:52.820 --> 57:57.080
Because to an outside observer, a viral meme looks exactly like massive convergence.

57:57.620 --> 58:02.240
Suddenly, millions of people are making the exact same hyper-specific joke about a TV show

58:02.240 --> 58:03.660
on the exact same day.

58:03.660 --> 58:06.780
It looks like the admissibility field forcing a thought.

58:07.340 --> 58:12.040
But in reality, the internet is the most traceable, heavily documented environment in

58:12.040 --> 58:12.680
human history.

58:12.840 --> 58:13.300
Exactly.

58:13.960 --> 58:18.060
Dedicated digital archivists can perform forensic reconstruction of a meme spread.

58:18.640 --> 58:23.840
They can trace timestamps, class platform posts, and digital screenshots back through the network

58:23.840 --> 58:26.760
to a literal patient zero, the first person to hit post.

58:27.100 --> 58:30.840
The transmission chain is fully intact, visible, and quantifiable.

58:30.840 --> 58:34.540
Therefore, L lineage overwhelmingly defeats L.A.

58:35.240 --> 58:40.400
It would be statistically ridiculous to claim that 10,000 isolated people independently invented

58:40.400 --> 58:45.400
the exact same distracted boyfriend meme format with the exact same text overlays on

58:45.400 --> 58:46.660
the exact same Tuesday.

58:46.900 --> 58:49.760
It's the exact same logic used in textual stomatics, right?

58:49.920 --> 58:52.880
Like when historians look at ancient, hand-copied manuscripts.

58:53.420 --> 58:53.740
Yes.

58:54.220 --> 58:58.840
Textual stomatics is the science of tracing manuscript lineages before the printing press.

58:58.840 --> 59:05.060
If two medieval monks in different monasteries hundreds of miles apart copy the same massive

59:05.060 --> 59:11.380
book and they both make the exact same highly specific, utterly bizarre spelling error on page

59:11.380 --> 59:17.760
42, the mathematical probability that they independently made that exact same random error due to environmental

59:17.760 --> 59:19.800
constraints is astronomically low.

59:19.800 --> 59:26.980
The lineage hypothesis that one monk physically copied the flawed text of the other, or they both copied from a shared

59:26.980 --> 59:29.480
flawed master text, is vastly more probable.

59:29.720 --> 59:30.100
Exactly.

59:30.380 --> 59:33.740
So the admissibility field explanation is not just a magic wand.

59:33.880 --> 59:38.300
You get to wave to sound smart and dismiss obvious plagiarism or direct inheritance.

59:38.680 --> 59:40.340
It has to earn its keep.

59:40.460 --> 59:46.680
It only becomes the dominant explanation when a transmission channel can be definitively scientifically ruled out.

59:46.680 --> 59:51.160
Like Darwin and Wallace, where the physical distance of the oceans made contact impossible.

59:51.660 --> 59:58.480
Or Lenski's E. coli, where 30,000 generations of evolutionary time rendered the genetic ancestor causally irrelevant.

59:58.800 --> 01:00:04.660
Or Carsonization, where the evolutionary distance between crabs is tens of millions of years wide.

01:00:04.900 --> 01:00:10.820
You have to rigorously eliminate the simple explanation of copying before you are allowed to invoke the profound

01:00:10.820 --> 01:00:12.880
structural explanation of the field.

01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:13.880
It is a discipline.

01:00:14.540 --> 01:00:18.360
It prevents Flyxion's theory from becoming an unfalsifiable dogma.

01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:25.140
The argument survives and thrives, specifically because it humbly admits its own boundaries.

01:00:25.560 --> 01:00:29.180
Okay, we have covered an immense amount of intellectual ground today.

01:00:29.580 --> 01:00:32.940
Let's take a deep breath and look back down the mountain we just climbed.

01:00:32.940 --> 01:00:39.520
We started in a microscopic laboratory flask, watching 30,000 generations of bacteria prove

01:00:39.520 --> 01:00:44.320
that sharing an ancestor doesn't mean sharing a destiny, and that origin doesn't equal explanation.

01:00:44.880 --> 01:00:49.380
We dove into the benthic depths of the ocean to watch nature obsessively try to build armored

01:00:49.380 --> 01:00:52.600
crabs out of whatever random crustacean parts were lying around.

01:00:53.040 --> 01:00:58.180
We sat in the hushed halls of the Linnean Society in 1858, as Darwin and Wallace arrived at

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:02.580
the exact same world-changing thought without ever speaking a single word to each other.

01:01:02.940 --> 01:01:09.240
We watched ancient, isolated civilizations independently plant seeds in the earth, not because they

01:01:09.240 --> 01:01:13.940
were taught to by a traveler, but because the melty ice and the changing climate demanded

01:01:13.940 --> 01:01:14.160
it.

01:01:14.220 --> 01:01:19.080
And we stood in the bitter halls of the royal society, watching Isaac Newton frantically try

01:01:19.080 --> 01:01:24.180
to prove he was the first man to look at a clock, while totally ignoring the invisible

01:01:24.180 --> 01:01:26.740
mathematical field that was actually guiding his hands.

01:01:26.740 --> 01:01:31.700
Through all of this, we have successfully, rigorously separated when something appears

01:01:31.700 --> 01:01:36.440
the historical origin from why it was able to appear at all the explanatory origin.

01:01:36.440 --> 01:01:42.080
And by doing so, we have recovered a much deeper, much more fascinating view of reality.

01:01:42.380 --> 01:01:47.800
A reality where similarities are not always just lazy echoes of the past or copies of copies,

01:01:48.040 --> 01:01:52.920
but are often robust, independent arrivals at the same deep structural truths of the universe.

01:01:52.920 --> 01:01:56.760
And that brings us to our final thought for this deep dive, something for you to chew

01:01:56.760 --> 01:01:59.100
on after you take your headcones off and go back to your day.

01:02:00.000 --> 01:02:05.820
We spent this whole time talking about bacteria and crabs and Victorian scientists and calculus,

01:02:06.200 --> 01:02:08.160
but what does this mean for you personally?

01:02:08.580 --> 01:02:11.960
Think about your own internal life, your own original thoughts.

01:02:12.300 --> 01:02:12.780
Exactly.

01:02:13.300 --> 01:02:18.480
When you are sitting at your desk at work or taking a shower or working on a creative hobby

01:02:18.480 --> 01:02:23.220
in your garage and you suddenly have a brilliant lightning bolt aha moment,

01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:27.520
a totally new way to solve a logistical problem at the office,

01:02:28.080 --> 01:02:30.140
a new melody for a song you're writing,

01:02:30.820 --> 01:02:34.560
a sudden profound new perspective on a difficult relationship.

01:02:35.060 --> 01:02:40.680
Our society trains you to view yourself as the singular historical origin of that thought.

01:02:40.940 --> 01:02:43.340
You are the lone genius who birthed it into the void.

01:02:43.520 --> 01:02:46.440
But what happens if you look at your own mind through Flyxion's lens?

01:02:46.440 --> 01:02:49.060
You have to ask yourself a very humbling question.

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:52.380
Are you the singular historical origin of that idea?

01:02:52.540 --> 01:02:56.400
Or are you simply the first local trajectory to hit the exact right coordinates

01:02:56.400 --> 01:03:00.680
in a shared admissibility field that we are all constantly collectively walking through?

01:03:00.940 --> 01:03:03.720
Did you magically invent the idea out of thin air?

01:03:03.900 --> 01:03:07.480
Or did your specific life experiences, the media you consume,

01:03:07.600 --> 01:03:10.340
your work environment, and your unique set of past choices,

01:03:10.340 --> 01:03:16.020
simply position you, like the clone who dropped the flashlight perfectly to discover something

01:03:16.020 --> 01:03:19.820
that was already waiting to be found in the structure of the problem itself?

01:03:19.980 --> 01:03:21.860
It strips away a little bit of the ego, sure.

01:03:22.000 --> 01:03:22.600
It does.

01:03:22.700 --> 01:03:26.020
But to me, it replaces that ego with something much more beautiful.

01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:32.660
It is a deeply humbling yet profoundly connected way to view human innovation and personal creativity.

01:03:32.660 --> 01:03:36.120
You aren't a lone genius shouting into an empty void.

01:03:36.700 --> 01:03:42.460
You are an explorer mapping an invisible landscape of possibilities that belongs to all of us.

01:03:42.980 --> 01:03:44.880
And sometimes, if you're lucky and you pay attention,

01:03:45.140 --> 01:03:47.340
you just happen to be the first one to find the trapdoor.

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:52.320
A landscape where the solutions were quietly waiting for us long before we ever arrived to find them.

01:03:52.320 --> 01:03:57.260
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive into Flyxion's incredible work.

01:03:57.920 --> 01:04:01.800
We're going to leave you here to mull over the invisible fields shaping your own ideas

01:04:01.800 --> 01:04:04.940
and maybe look at the crabs at the beach with a little more respect.

01:04:05.480 --> 01:04:09.100
Keep questioning the origins of things and we'll catch you on the next deep dive.

